**Endometriosis** is a long-term condition where **tissue similar to the lining of the womb** grows in other places, such as the ovaries, fallopian tubes, bladder or bowel. Each month this tissue reacts to hormones and bleeds, which can cause pain, inflammation and scarring. It affects around **1 in 10 women** and people assigned female at birth.

## Symptoms

- **severe period pain** that stops you doing normal activities
- **pelvic pain** at other times of the month
- pain during or after **sex**
- pain when pooing or peeing, especially during your period
- heavy periods
- extreme **tiredness**
- difficulty getting pregnant

Symptoms vary a lot. Some people with mild endometriosis have severe pain, and some with severe endometriosis have few symptoms.

## Getting diagnosed

- talk to your GP and keep a **pain and symptom diary**
- you may have a **pelvic examination** and an **ultrasound scan**. A normal scan does not rule it out
- you may be referred to a gynaecologist. The only way to be certain is **laparoscopy**, keyhole surgery to look inside

Read about [endometriosis treatment](/conditions/kidney-bladder-and-reproductive/endometriosis/treatment/).
